{start_of_verse label="Verse 1"}
Near [Em]Banbridge town, in the [D]County [Em]Down,
One [Em]morning in [D]July,
Down a [Em]boreen green
Came a [G]sweet col[D]leen,
And she [Em]smiled as she [D]passed me [Em]by.
She [Em]looked so sweet from her [D]two bare [Em]feet
To the [Em]sheen of her [D]nut-brown hair,
Such a [Em]coaxing elf,
I'd to [G]shake my[D]self
To make [Em]sure I was [D]standing [Em]there.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
{x_strum: D - D U D U}
From [G]Bantry Bay up to [D]Derry [Em]Quay,
And from [Em]Galway to [D]Dublin town,
No [Em]maid I've seen like the [G]sweet col[D]leen
That I [Em]met in the [D]County [Em]Down.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 2"}
As she [Em]onward sped I [D]shook my [Em]head
And I [Em]gazed with a [D]feeling rare,
And I [Em]said, says I,
To a [G]passer-[D]by,
'Who's the [Em]maid with the [D]nut-brown [Em]hair?'
He [Em]smiled at me, and with [D]pride says [Em]he,
'That's the [Em]gem of [D]Ireland's crown.
She's young [Em]Rosie McCann
From the [G]banks of the [D]Bann,
She's the [Em]star of the [D]County [Em]Down.'
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 3"}
At the [Em]harvest fair she'll be [D]surely [Em]there
And I'll [Em]dress in my [D]Sunday clothes,
With my [Em]shoes shone bright
And my [G]hat cocked [D]right
For a [Em]smile from my [D]nut-brown [Em]rose.
No [Em]pipe I'll smoke, no [D]horse I'll [Em]yoke
Till my [Em]plough turns [D]rust colour brown,
Till a [Em]smiling bride
By my [G]own fire[D]side
Sits the [Em]star of the [D]County [Em]Down.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}
{x_end: Em}
